        "thinking": "I'm working through the curadi root छदि (meaning संवरणे), which takes णिच् to form छन्दयति. Now I need to figure out the order of operations when combining यङ्, णिच्, and सन् affixes, applying them innermost-out starting from यङ्.\n\nFor यङ् I get चाछन्द्य by reduplicating with abhyāsa guṇa (छ→च, dīrgha a→ā), since छन्द् is ekāc halādi. Adding णिच् next, the sequence of deletions (ato lopaḥ, then yasya halaḥ) yields चाछन्दि, with no vṛddhi since the अ isn't truly upadhā here. Then applying सन् with iṭ (since it's seṭ after ṇic), I'm working out the guṇa and reduplication steps to arrive at something like चाछन्दयिषति, carefully tracking how the reduplication of सन् interacts with the ṇijanta form.\n\nNow I'm checking whether सन् causes a second reduplication on top of the yaṅ's existing abhyāsa — comparing against known examples like बोभूयिषते (yaṅanta + san) to see if further dvitva applies here.\n\nI'm trying to recall why no second reduplication occurs once the yaṅanta has already undergone abhyāsa — the double reduplication doesn't reapply to the san-pratyaya formation. This confirms my sense that बोभूयिषते is the standard attested form without further duplication.\n\nMoving to the next verb, I'm working through चाछन्दयिष् as a sannanta, where since the san follows the ṇic causative, the parasmaipada ending follows from the णिचश्च rule.\n\nI'm working through whether tuk applies before छ in the abhyāsa: since carca converts jhal to car/jaś turning छ to च, and the dīrgha lengthening (7.4.83) makes the abhyāsa long, tuk shouldn't apply because that rule targets hrasva. I need to check the relative ordering of these rules, since tuk insertion (6.1.73) and dīrgha lengthening (7.4.83) might interact via asiddhatva.\n\nI'm checking the parallel case with प्रच्छ् in yaṅ to confirm the pattern, recalling चिच्छेद in liṭ as the model with short abhyāsa plus tuk. Applying the same logic to छन्द्: dvitva gives छ+छन्द्, then tuk inserts before the hrasva abhyāsa vowel yielding छत्छन्द्य, and then the abhyāsa vowel should lengthen to ā in the next step.\n\nWorking through the resulting form छात्छन्द्य, I apply the carca-conditioned change of छ to च giving चात्छन्द्य, then श्चुत्व converts that to चाच्छन्द्य, suggesting the final yaṅ form चाच्छन्द्यते. I'm now questioning whether tuk is siddha relative to dīrgha since they aren't in the tripādī and thus could interact — if dīrgha applied first, tuk wouldn't trigger, but if tuk applied first dīrgha would still apply, which tells me tuk must be anitya while dīrgha is nitya.\n\nBut comparing this to चेच्छिद्यते, I notice guṇa of the abhyāsa is also treated as nitya, yet the attested form still shows तुक् applying, so I'm trying to recall whether Whitney's grammar lists this intensive form of चिद् as cecchidyate, and whether there's an antaraṅga/bahiraṅga principle resolving why तुक् applies here despite nitya operations.\n\nI'm trying to recall how vidyut-prakriya sequences its rules, specifically whether tuk insertion (6.1.73) happens before or after abhyāsa lengthening—if the dīrgha happens first, there'd be no tuk, giving चाछिद् rather than the expected form with tuk inserted.\n\nActually I remember now: 6.1.75 \"दीर्घात्\" is the relevant sūtra for tuk after a long vowel, giving examples like चेच्छिद्यते, आच्छादयति, म्लेच्छति—so tuk does apply even after dīrgha abhyāsa.\n\nSo I get चाच्छन्द्य, and with सम् the full desiderative form becomes सञ्चाच्छन्दयिषाम् (or संचाच्छन्दयिषाम्, both valid depending on whether anusvāra converts to ञ्). I'll settle on सञ्चाच्छन्दयिषाम्.\n\nFor the ṇic derivation: चाच्छन्द्य + णिच् loses the 'a' via ato lopaḥ since ārdhadhātuka follows, then yasya halaḥ deletes the य after the consonant, giving चाच्छन्द् + इ, with no vṛddhi since the upadhā isn't 'a'.\n\nMoving to the san stage: चाच्छन्दि + san takes iṭ since the ṇijanta is seṭ, yielding चाच्छन्दयिष्. I'm weighing whether a second reduplication applies here — analogous to forms like ब